  "query_preview": "(This evening through Monday)...\nIssued at 256 PM EDT Mon Jul 27 2026\n\nSatellite and radar imagery shows a large complex of showers and \nthunderstorms over the Chicago area as of 2pm. This cluster of \nstorms is likely going to impact our area to some extent this \nafternoon and evening. At the surface, a distinct axis of greater \ninstability (3000-4000 J/Kg MLCAPE) extends NW to SE through most of \ncentral Indiana. Surface winds are generally southwesterly to the \nwest of this boundary. Along it, light and variable conditions are \nnoted. To the east, a southeasterly wind is observed.\n\nAloft, strong southeasterly shear between 45-55kt extends over most \nof Illinois, Indiana and Ohio. This mean shear vector is almost \nexactly parallel to the instability axis. Further to the north, a \npotent mid-level vort max is shown which was the catalyst to ongoing \nconvection. Now that storms have initiated, the immediate mesoscale \nconcern is how storms evolve coming into our CWA in the next few \nhours.\n\nOne scenario is that the storms develop a strong cold pool and begin \npropagating southeastward along the instability axis. This is \nplausible since the shear vector points southeastward along this \naxis, and cold pool shear balance will be easier to achieve. In this \nscenario, severe winds will be the primary hazard. However, embedded \nsupercells, as have been seen over Chicago this afternoon, would be \ncapable of all hazards including large hail and tornadoes. \nAdditionally, a QLCS tornado threat may develop if the low-level jet \nramps up significantly this evening.\n\nA second scenario is similar to the one above, but the low-level jet \nramps up a bit earlier and storms propagate more southward owing to \ngreater upshear growth. This would send the storm cluster over our \nwestern CWA with similar hazards to scenario one. All in all, the \nmain difference is timing and influence from the low-level jet. \nGuidance shows the low-level jet increasing substantially around 02z \nto between 30-40kt at 925mb. However, the storms may already be \nthrough our area by that point. Renewed development on the western \nedge could occur if a residual cold pool lingers over the area. \nFlooding could occur if storms begin redeveloping over the same \nareas as the initial round of activity.\n\nAfter tonight, the vort max and associated surface low depart \neastward with a cold front sweeping through by dawn. Northern winds \nusher in cooler and drier air with temperatures falling towards \nnormal for the remainder of the week. Another vort max may arrive \nthis weekend, embedded within weak westerlies aloft, bringing \nprecipitation chances back by Friday. Given the weak flow and \nlikewise weak vort max it would seem prudent to keep precipitation \nchances capped at chance (40 percent). Model uncertainty is high, and \nsuch weak/subtle features can be hard to pin point this far out.",
